s="\"Mixed metric dimension of $2$-connected graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.27573)\\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; We show that for a $2$-connected graph $G$ which is not a cycle, the mixed metric dimension of $G$ is at most $2c(G)$, where $c(G)$ is cyclomatic number of $G$. As an immediate application, we prove a conjecture proposed by Sedlar and Škrekovski.\\nTitle: Mixed metric dimension of $2$-connected graphs\\nAuthors: Shi Chen, Xuanlong Ma\\nDate: Thu Jul 30 01:29:42 2026\\n\\nWe show that for a $2$-connected graph $G$ which is not a cycle, the mixed metric dimension of $G$ is at most $2c(G)$, where $c(G)$ is cyclomatic number of $G$. As an immediate application, we prove a conjecture proposed by Sedlar and Škrekovski.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nExtremal mixed metric dimension with respect to the cyclomatic number (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300321003283)\\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # Extremal mixed metric dimension with respect to the cyclomatic number ... $ As the number of cycles in trees and graphs with edge disjoint cycles equals the cyclomatic number, this lead the authors of [12] to make the following conjecture.\\n\\n## Applied Mathematics and Computation\\n\\nVolume 404, 1 September 2021, 126238\\n\\n# Extremal mixed metric dimension with respect to the cyclomatic number\\n\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.amc.2021.126238Get rights and content\\n\\n## Highlights\\n\\n  * •\\n\\nProve that for unbalanced $\\\\Theta $-graphs the strict inequality holds.\\n\\n  * •\\n\\nProve that for balanced $\\\\Theta $-graphs the equality is attained.\\n\\n  * •\\n\\nFurther conjecture that balanced $\\\\Theta $-graphs, besides trees and cactus graph in which every cycle has precisely one vertex of degree $\\\\ge $ 3 for which it was already known that equality holds, are the only graphs for which this upper bound is attained.\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nIn a graph $G,$ the cardinality of the smallest ordered set of vertices that distinguishes every element of $V\\\\left(G\\\\right)\\\\cup E\\\\left(G\\\\right)$ is called the mixed metric dimension of $G,$ and it is denoted by $\\\\mathrm{mdim}\\\\left(G\\\\right).$ In [12] it was conjectured that every graph $G$ with cyclomatic number $c\\\\left(G\\\\right)$ satisfies $\\\\mathrm{mdim}\\\\left(G\\\\right)\\\\le {L}_{1}\\\\left(G\\\\right)+2c\\\\left(G\\\\right)$ where ${L}_{1}\\\\left(G\\\\right)$ is the number of leaves in $G$. Even before, this notion was introduced for the realm of metric spaces [1]. The concept of vertex metric dimension [[2], [7], [10]] was recently extended from resolving vertices to resolving edges of a graph by Kelenc et al. [5], which lead to the definition of the edge metric dimension. Finally, it was further extended to resolving mixed pairs of edges and vertices by Kelenc et al. [6] which resulted with the notion of the mixed metric dimension. All these variations of metric dimensions attracted interest (see [[8], [9], [11], [12], [14], [15], [16]]), while for a wider and systematic introduction of the topic metric dimension that encapsulates all three above mentioned variations, we recommend the Ph.D. thesis of Kelenc [4].\\n\\nIn literature, among other questions, the mixed metric dimension of trees, unicyclic graphs and graphs with edge disjoint cycles was studied. Denoting by ${L}_{1}\\\\left(G\\\\right)$ the number of leaves in a graph $G,$ we first cite the following result from [6].\\n\\nProposition 1\\n\\nFor every tree $T,$ it holds$\\\\mathrm{mdim}\\\\left(T\\\\right)={L}_{1}\\\\left(T\\\\right).$\\n\\nA graph in which all cycles are pairwise edge disjoint is called a cactus graph. Having that in mind, the following results were proven in [12], first for unicyclic graphs and after for all cactus graphs.\\n\\nTheorem 2\\n\\nLet $G\\\\ne {C}_{n}$ be a cactus graph with $c$ cycles. Then$\\\\mathrm{mdim}\\\\left(G\\\\right)\\\\le {L}_{1}\\\\left(G\\\\right)+2c,$and the upper bound is attained if and only if every cycle in $G$ has exactly one vertex of degree $\\\\ge 3$.\\n\\nThe cyclomatic number of a graph $G$ is defined by $c\\\\left(G\\\\right)=m-n+1.$ As the number of cycles in trees and graphs with edge disjoint cycles equals the cyclomatic number, this lead the authors of [12] to make the following conjecture.\\n\\nConjecture 3\\n\\nLet $G\\\\ne {C}_{n}$ be a graph, $c\\\\left(G\\\\right)$ its cyclomatic number, and ${L}_{1}\\\\left(G\\\\right)$ the number of leaves in $G$. Then$\\\\mathrm{mdim}\\\\left(G\\\\right)\\\\le {L}_{1}\\\\left(G\\\\right)+2c\\\\left(G\\\\right).$\\n\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nGraphs attaining an upper bound on the mixed metric dimension (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17944)\\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Given a graph $G$, we show that the mixed metric dimension of $G$ is exactly $\\\\ell(G)+2c(G)$ if and only if $G$ is either a cactus graph in which every cycle has precisely one vertex of degree at least $3$, or a balanced $Θ$-graph, where $\\\\ell(G)$ and $c(G)$ denote the number of leaves and the cyclomatic number of $G$, respectively. This provides an affirmative answer to a conjecture proposed by Sedlar and Škrekovski (2021).\\nTitle: Graphs attaining an upper bound on the mixed metric dimension\\nAuthors: Shi Chen, Xuanlong Ma\\nDate: Tue Aug 18 16:01:23 2026\\n\\nGiven a graph $G$, we show that the mixed metric dimension of $G$ is exactly $\\\\ell(G)+2c(G)$ if and only if $G$ is either a cactus graph in which every cycle has precisely one vertex of degree at least $3$, or a balanced $Θ$-graph, where $\\\\ell(G)$ and $c(G)$ denote the number of leaves and the cyclomatic number of $G$, respectively. This provides an affirmative answer to a conjecture proposed by Sedlar and Škrekovski (2021).----------------